course: biology
lesson: 256
title: The microscopic world: cells, the building blocks of life
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every living thing is built from cells, tiny living bricks far too small to see with your eyes alone; you are made of tens of trillions of them.
explain: Put one drop of pond water under a school microscope and a whole hidden world swims into view: slipper-shaped paramecia rowing with tiny hairs, amoebas flowing like blobs of jelly, green algae, each of them just one single cell.
explain: A single cell can eat, move and make copies of itself by splitting in two; that is how your scraped knee heals, as new skin cells divide and fill the gap while the scab protects the wound on top.
explain: Inside each cell's nucleus is DNA, a long instruction book that tells the cell what to do; stretched out, the DNA of one cell would be about two metres long, yet it is so thin and so tightly coiled that it fits into a space far smaller than a full stop.
explain: Cartoons sometimes show cells as little people with faces and jobs; real cells have no faces or brains, but each kind really does have its own job, like carrying oxygen, sensing light or making a muscle move.
fact: Antonie van Leeuwenhoek first saw tiny living things in water, which he called «animalcules», with homemade lenses in the 1670s.
fact: In 1665 Robert Hooke looked at a slice of cork, saw little boxes and called them cells, like the small rooms of monks; they were the dead walls of plant cells.
fact: Hooke's book Micrographia amazed readers with huge drawings of fleas and lice.
fact: Plant cells have a stiff wall, a nucleus and, in green parts, chloroplasts that catch light; iodine stain makes the nucleus show as a dark dot.
fact: Animal cells, like the soft roundish cells scraped from your cheek, have a thin skin called a membrane instead of a stiff wall.
fact: Scientists estimate that an adult body has about 30 to 37 trillion cells of about 200 kinds, and most of them are red blood cells.
fact: The yolk of an unfertilised hen's egg is often called a single cell, one of the largest there is, full of food for a growing chick.
fact: Nerve cells carry messages fast from one part of the body to another, and some are very long; one runs from the bottom of your spine to your big toe, about a metre, and a giraffe has nerve cells several metres long.
fact: Cells make copies of themselves by splitting in two, and your body makes millions of new cells every second.
fact: You lose many thousands of skin cells every minute, and much of house dust is dead skin.
fact: Each cell's DNA, stretched out, would be about two metres long, and people share many basic DNA instructions even with a banana.
fact: A paramecium, or slipper animal, swims with thousands of tiny hairs called cilia that beat like oars; when it bumps into something, it beats them the other way, backs off and swims off in a new direction.
fact: An amoeba moves by flowing its inside forward into a bulge called a false foot, and it swallows food by flowing around it.
fact: Electron microscopes use beams of electrons instead of light and show things thousands of times smaller than a light microscope can, such as the spiky shapes of pollen grains.
exercise: What did Antonie van Leeuwenhoek see in a drop of water in the 1670s? | Tiny living things he called «animalcules» | Nothing at all but clear, clean water | The atoms that water is made of, in neat rows | Little fish too small to catch
exercise: Why did Robert Hooke use the word «cell»? | The little boxes in cork looked like the small rooms of monks | Cells were named after the prison cells where germs were kept | He found them in a battery cell | He thought they were tiny eggs
exercise: What was Hooke actually looking at in the cork? | The dead walls of plant cells | Living animals swimming in the cork | Tiny bubbles of air in water | The roots of a cork tree
exercise: Which part do plant cells have but animal cells do not? | A stiff cell wall | A nucleus | A membrane | DNA
exercise: Why do teachers add iodine to onion skin on a slide? | It makes the nucleus show up as a dark dot | It kills all the germs living on the onion | It makes the onion cells grow bigger | It turns the cells bright green so they show up
exercise: What surrounds an animal cell, such as a cheek cell? | A thin skin called a membrane | A stiff wall like the wood in a tree | A hard shell of glass | Nothing at all
exercise: About how many cells are in an adult human body? | About 30 to 37 trillion | About 200 | About a million | About 30 thousand
exercise: Which kind of cell is the most common in your body? | Red blood cells | Nerve cells | Skin cells | Bone cells
exercise: What is the yolk of an unfertilised hen's egg often called? | One single, very large cell | A bag of millions of cells | A baby chick that has not grown | A ball of fat with no cells
exercise: About how long is the nerve cell from the bottom of your spine to your big toe? | About a metre | About a millimetre | About a centimetre | About ten metres
exercise: How do cells make new cells? | By splitting in two | By growing out of the blood | By being made in the stomach | By melting together
exercise: What is DNA? | A long instruction book in each cell's nucleus | The food that a cell eats every single day to keep itself alive | The water inside a cell | The thick wall around a plant cell
exercise: About how long would one cell's DNA be if stretched out? | About two metres | About two millimetres | About two kilometres | About two centimetres
exercise: How does a paramecium swim? | With thousands of tiny hairs called cilia | With one long whippy tail, like a tadpole's | By flowing into a false foot | By jumping along on lots of tiny legs
exercise: How does an amoeba move? | By flowing its inside forward into a false foot | By rowing with tiny hairs | By rolling like a ball | By swimming along with tiny fins and a tail, like a fish
exercise: What can an electron microscope do that a school microscope cannot? | Show things thousands of times smaller | Show things in bright natural colours | Look at living animals in the dark | See through walls and doors
exercise: Why does a scraped knee heal? | New skin cells divide and fill the gap | The scab slowly hardens and turns into brand-new skin | Blood sets hard into skin | The bone grows over the cut
test: «my brother says we're made of 100 cells, like big lego bricks» What is right? | No, about 30 trillion or more, far too small to see | Yes, about a hundred big ones that you could count | No, just one giant cell | No, people are made of atoms, not cells, so we have none
test: In a cartoon about the body, cells are little people with faces who chat and argue. What is true about real cells? | They have no faces or brains, but each kind has its own job | They talk to each other in little words, just like people do at work | They wear tiny uniforms for their jobs | They are all exactly the same
test: «a chicken egg is one cell?? no way, it's massive» What is right? | Yes, an unfertilised yolk is often called a single, very large cell | No, it is made of about a million tiny cells, all packed tightly in together | No, eggs are not alive at all | Yes, but only brown eggs from hens that live on farms
test: Count them: under the microscope Leo counts 3 amoebas and 5 slipper animals. How many single cells is that? | 8 | 15 | 5 | 3
test: What would you see if you looked at a plant leaf cell and your cheek cell side by side? | The leaf cell has a stiff wall and green chloroplasts; the cheek cell does not | They would look exactly the same, because all living cells are alike inside, plant or animal | The cheek cell would be green | The leaf cell would have no nucleus
test: «i'll pick my scab, it's fine» Why is that not a good idea? | The scab protects the wound while new cells fill the gap | Scabs are needed to make new blood for the body | Picking a scab makes the bone break | The scab is the place where all of your new skin cells are made, then sent round the body
test: How can two metres of DNA fit in a tiny cell? | It is extremely thin and tightly packed and coiled | It is folded once in half | It sticks out of the cell like a very long, thin tail | It is only two metres in giants
test: Why did no one know about tiny living things in water before the 1600s? | Without microscopes they were too small to see | They did not exist before then | People thought looking at water was rude | Water was always boiled before
test: «we share DNA with a banana lol so we're half banana» What is right? | We share many basic cell instructions, but that does not make us part banana | Yes, we are exactly half banana inside, which is why so many people like to eat them | No, bananas have no DNA at all | Yes, that is why monkeys like bananas
test: A paramecium bumps into a stone. With no brain, what can it do? | Reverse its tiny hairs and swim another way | Nothing, it stays stuck forever | Grow a brain to think about it | Call for help from all the other cells close by
test: Why might a nerve cell need to be so long? | To carry messages fast all the way from one place to another | Because it is the oldest cell | To hold up the leg like a strong bone, from the hip down to the toe | Because long cells store more food
exam: Identify the investigator who, in the year 1665, first applied the term «cell» to structures observed within cork. | Robert Hooke | Antonie van Leeuwenhoek | Charles Darwin | Louis Pasteur
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the difference between plant and animal cells. | Plant cells possess a rigid cell wall, whereas animal cells are bounded solely by a membrane | Animal cells possess a rigid wall, whereas plant cells possess none at all | Only animal cells contain a nucleus | Plant and animal cells are in all respects identical, differing only in their colour and in their size
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which an organism replaces cells lost through injury? | Existing cells divide into two, thereby generating new cells | New cells are formed afresh from the material of the scab as it hardens | Cells are imported from the blood in finished form | Lost cells are never replaced
exam: Ascertain the approximate number of cells of which an adult human body is composed, according to current scientific estimates. | Between thirty and thirty-seven trillion | Approximately two hundred | Approximately one million | Precisely one hundred thousand, as counted by microscope
